Oh no! You're trying to run your React component in the render body, but you haven't used useEffect yet! UseEffect is like a magic potion that makes your component's state update automatically when it's called from anywhere in the application. It's like having a secret agent who always knows what's going on in your codebase!

But don't worry, we can fix this! We need to add an arrow function to our useEffect hook. Here's how:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  // Your code here...
}, [your-component]);
```

Now, whenever your component is rendered, it will call useEffect with `[your-component]` as its argument. This way, your component's state will be updated automatically, and your page won't freeze anymore!

Remember, useEffect is like a superhero cape for your code! It's the key to making sure everything runs smoothly and efficiently!
